<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leitha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Leitha is a river about 180 kilometres long that flows from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, where it joins the Moson arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. For much of its course it formed the historical border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Hungary. After 1867 the two halves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> were informally called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisleithania">Cisleithania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Transleithania</a> after it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leitha.jpg" alt="Leitha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Glock · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leitha_Kleinwolkersdorf.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Leitha is a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, with a basin of 2,138 square kilometres. It rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> where its two headstreams meet: the Schwarza, which drains the Schneeberg, Rax and Schneealpe ranges of the Northern Limestone Alps, and the Pitten. Parts of its course form the border between Lower Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a>. East of Nickelsdorf it passes into Hungary and flows into the Moson arm of the Danube near Mosonmagyaróvár. Large amounts of Schwarza water are diverted to the Wiener Neustadt Canal and to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> with drinking water, and in places the river bed is almost dry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river was first mentioned as the Lithaha in an 833 deed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, in the Carolingian Avar March. Its name probably comes from a Pannonian word for mud. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> horsemen were defeated by King Otto I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955, the forces of the Bavarian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Henry the Wrangler</a> gradually reconquered the lands up to the Leitha, where about 976 the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was established under the Babenberg margrave Leopold I. Around the turn to the second millennium the Hungarian frontier ran along the river, and from 1156 it formed the eastern border of the Duchy of Austria, with fortresses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>, Bruck and Hainburg.</p><p>The last Babenberg duke, Frederick II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was killed in 1246 in the Battle of the Leitha River against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a>. A deed of King Sigismund in 1411 confirmed the border when his daughter Elizabeth married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-austria">Albert II of Austria</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</a> the Viennese called Hungary Transleithania, beyond the Leitha, and the Austrian lands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisleithania">Cisleithania</a>, on this side of it. These names reflected the Austrian view, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> lay on this side. The river did not form the whole border: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, part of Cisleithania, lay north-east of Hungary, and the Morava separated Cisleithanian Moravia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the West Hungarian territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a>, and the river became an internal Austrian border.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leitha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 October 1921 their commander, Pál Prónay, proclaimed the independent Banate of <b>Leitha</b> at Oberwart.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-leitha">Banate of Leitha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babenberg margraves pushed the frontier east to the March and the <b>Leitha</b>, encouraging settlement and founding monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
